From cggreen at u.washington.edu  Fri Apr  1 23:09:52 2005
From: cggreen at u.washington.edu (Christopher Green)
Date: Fri, 01 Apr 2005 15:09:52 -0800
Subject: SV4 class design question/storage of large objects in S
Message-ID: <20050401230732.61CFD100850D@mailgate.biostat.wustl.edu>

Hello all,
 
I am designing several SV4 data structures for a financial application.  The
data structures can be rather large, large enough that unnecessarily copying
them, etc. could be troublesome (e.g. we might run out of memory).
 
I want to design a set of iterator classes for these data structures to
simplify common end user operations.  For example, one might want to pull
out a "slice" at a time, and do something with said slice.
 
There are different data structures for handling different data sources
(data files, databases, etc.).  I am writing the iterator classes to hide
the messy data source details from the end user.  (That is, the end user can
grab a conceptual "slice" without having to worry about the actual
representation of the data.)
 
Now in C++, the iterator class (as I would design it) would have a pointer
to the actual object over which the iteration is being performed.  There are
no pointers in S, so things seem more complicated (to me)...
 
Right now, this what I have in mind for the design.  It is possible, though
very unlikely, that the object over which we want to iterator will change
while we are iterating. (The iterator itself will not change the object,
however.) So my iterator class constructor has a logical argument called
"safe" that indicates whether this concerns us.
 
        -If safe is true, the iterator object should store a copy of the
data structure object.  If the original data structure object changes, the
iterator still has the "correct" object.

        -If safe is false, we don't *need* to make a copy, as the user is
telling us the data structure will not be changed during iteration (and we
are trusting the user not to do so).  We could either make the copy, or
store the name of the object and "get" it when we actually need it.

Now, my understanding (if I am reading the Green Book correctly) of argument
passing and copying is that no actual copying will be done until an object
changes---only reference counts will change.  So "storing" a copy of the
object won't take up any additional space, so long as the object doesn't
change.  In particular, 

        in the safe=T case, keeping a copy of the data structure just in
case is almost always a cheap operation, as the chance that the data
structure changes during iteration is small, but occasionally we pay a huge
price storagewise; and 
        in the safe=F case, we might as well keep a copy of the data
structure, as it does not require additional storage and is cleaner than
using "get" (which is arguably cheating).

Hopefully, I have explained this clearly enough.

My questions: 

1) Is my understanding of how S-PLUS will handle the copy correct?
2) Is there a better way to do this sort of thing? Another idea I had was to
add some sort of "lock" to the data structure itself, and make the
(dimensions of the) data structure "read-only" while the iteration is being
performed.

Any comments or suggestions are welcome.

Thanks,

Chris Green
 
 
