Subject: Plotting confidence intervals when using Kenv.label
Message-ID: <4268A292@TOP1S46>

I am not proficient with the S+ programming language so the answer to this 
question may be quite easy, but not obvious to me.  I have S+ 6.0 on Windows 
XP with the Splancs library.
I have a data set containing point locations for parasitized and unparasitized 
individuals and am using the difference in Ripley's K-functions between the 
two data points to determine if clustering occurs at smaller distances in the 
parasitized (following Diggle and Chetwynd 1991).  Basically I calculate the 
Kuu (K-function centered over an unparasitized individual, counting the other 
unparasitized) and the Kpp (same but for parasitized), then the difference is 
D=Kuu-Kpp.  Random labeling of the parasitized/unparasitized point locations 
is done using Kenv.label function in Splancs.  The simulation is run 999 
times, and the upper and lower bounds of these simulations are plotted along 
with my observed values to determine differences in clustering.  When the 
observed are below the bounds, parasitized individuals are clustered more 
tightly at those distances.  I'm happy with this.  BUT I would also like to 
plot the 95% confidence intervals for the random label simulations.  Any 
suggestions on how to do so?
Many thanks,
Rachel

Here is the script I am currently using:

cup <- read.table('C:/Data/c1up.dat',header=T)
cp <- read.table('C:/Data/c1p.dat',header=T)
cpoly <- list(x=c(cup$x, cp$x), y=c(cup$y, cp$y))
K1.hat <- khat(as.points(cup), bbox(as.points(cpoly)), seq(0,1000,10))
K2.hat <- khat(as.points(cp), bbox(as.points(cpoly)), seq(0,1000,10))
K.diff <- K1.hat-K2.hat
plot(seq(0,1000,10), K.diff, xlab="Distance", ylim=c(-100000,100000), 
type="l", main="Simulation envelopes, random labelling")
env.lab <- Kenv.label(as.points(cup), as.points(cp), bbox(as.points(cpoly)), 
nsim=99, s=seq(0,1000,10))
lines(seq(0,1000,10), env.lab$upper, lty=2)
lines(seq(0,1000,10), env.lab$lower, lty=2)

