I don't think it's completely fair to claim that having the integer 
value 2,146,435,073 associated with NA "makes no sense".  It's quite 
useful to have an NA value in the atomic number mode "integer".  Unless 
one is going to use more than 32 bits to represent 32 bit signed 
integers plus an NA value, one has to take one value out of the space of 
signed 32 bit integers to represent NA.  The designers of S-PLUS used 
the value 2^31 - 2^20 + 1 to represent integer NA.  The designers of R 
used the value -(2^31) to represent integer NA (I believe).  In 
hindsight, the latter is probably a better choice, but the practical 
implications are similar.

I was merely pointing this out because when you use S-PLUS integers in a 
way in which they were not designed to be used (which is not to say you 
shouldn't do that), you do need to be careful because one of the bit 
patterns gets treated specially by the rest of S-PLUS (so, you need to 
be careful about supplying the right flags to .C() and .Call(), etc.)

-- Tony Plate

