Hello.  I am attempting to re-write a simulation of someone elses 
written in SAS in S-Plus.  The simulation is to calculate power for a 
cluster randomized trial that is essentially a 2^2 factorial design.  
The outcome is binary.  The old program used NLMIXED to analyse the 
simulated data.  This is my first real foray into these type of models, 
so I'm on the learning curve.

Here is the code I was given. 

proc nlmixed qmax=31 data=test1;
 parms /data=perm.parmdata;
 eta = beta0 + beta1*arm1 + beta2*arm2 + beta3*arm3 + u;
 expeta = exp(eta);
 p = expeta/(1 + expeta);
 model outcomes ~ binomial(n,p);
 random u ~ normal(0,s2u) subject = doc;
 predict beta1 out=arm1 (keep = pred tvalue probt);
 predict beta2 out=arm2 (keep = pred tvalue probt);
 predict beta3 out=arm3 (keep = pred tvalue probt);
 predict beta3 - beta2 out=synergy (keep = pred tvalue probt);
run;

The dummy variables arm1-3 are those you would get from coding a single 
4-level categorical variable.

Here is the attempt at an equivalent call to glme I have used in S-Plus.

glme(cbind(y,m)~grp,data=sim,random=~1|doc,family=binomial)

Now, when I run these analyses on the same test data, I don't get 
identical parameter estimates, nor do I expect to since NLMIXED uses 
gaussian quadrature and glme uses penalized quasi-likelihood.  I tried 
to use the AGQUAD method in glme, but it bombed out.  Anyway, the 
parameter estimates are "close" between the two methods.

So, after that lengthy intro, my question is am I approaching this 
correctly?  Can the the NLMIXED analysis be done with glme or nlme or 
something else?

Kind Regards,

Kevin

