Sounds like the dataframe columns might be factor variables (although it 
is not unknown for there to be a bug in S-PLUS, a bug is a far less 
likely explanation than others available for what you are seeing).  With 
the read.table() function, this sort of thing happens if there are 
entries in a column that S-PLUS cannot interpret as numbers.  These 
columns print without quotes, so the columns still look like numbers, 
but when you try to do arithmetic on them you get NA.  To see if this is 
the case, do 'sapply(x, class)' at the command line (where 'x' is your 
dataframe), and look at the output to see if your columns are factor or 
numeric (they should be numeric).  To fix this, either fix your original 
data, or do something like this at the command line:

> # visually inspect this output to see if it makes sense
> cbind(levels(x$factor.col), as.numeric(levels(x$factor.col))
> table(is.na(x$factor.col))
> # convert! (this creates a new column, but you can overwrite the
> # original if you wish)
> x$numeric.col <- as.numeric(as.character(x$factor.col))

-- Tony Plate

