From tplate at blackmesacapital.com  Tue Apr 12 00:00:05 2005
From: tplate at blackmesacapital.com (Tony Plate)
Date: Mon, 11 Apr 2005 18:00:05 -0600
Subject: Re: another weird function
In-Reply-To: <54668D97C0199943A454516A5FF6481E58A962@EXCHUS001.AD.MLP.COM>
References: <54668D97C0199943A454516A5FF6481E58A962@EXCHUS001.AD.MLP.COM>
Message-ID: <425B0F85.60800@blackmesacapital.com>

This is "Last Observation Carried Forwards" with 99.99 standing for the 
NA value.  Here's a little function for LOCF:

locf <-
function(x, initial = NA)
{
        if(!is.na(initial) && length(x) && is.na(x[1]))
                x[1] <- initial
        x.pos <- which(!is.na(x))
        if(length(x.pos) == 0 || x.pos[1] != 1)
                x.pos <- c(1, x.pos)
        x[] <- rep(x[x.pos], c(diff(x.pos), length(x) - x.pos[length(x.pos)] + 
1))
        x
}

If you replace your 99.99 values by NA, then the above function will do 
as you specify:

> x <- c(22,      99.99,  31,  99.99,  99.99,  99.99)
> x
[1] 22.00 99.99 31.00 99.99 99.99 99.99
> x[x==99.99] <- NA
> locf(x)
[1] 22 22 31 31 31 31
> x <- c(99.99,   99.99,   31,   99.99,   99.99,   99.99)
> x[x==99.99] <- NA
> x
[1] NA NA 31 NA NA NA
> locf(x)
[1] NA NA 31 31 31 31
>

(and you can put the 99.99's back if you need to)

-- Tony Plate

