On Tue, 12 Apr 2005, Tim Hesterberg wrote:

> > I perform a jackknife estimation on a give statistic ``theta'' using
> > jackknife. And it return the replicates and estimate of theta.
> >
> >
> >
> > However, I don't know how to get the jackknife confidence interval for
> > theta.
>
> I'm not aware of anything known as a "jackknife confidence interval".

It's the same idea as the bootstrap basic interval using the 
pseudo-values. AFAIR it is described in Efron's SIAM lecture notes.

> Do you mean that you would like a t confidence interval using
> a jackknife standard error?  If so, then
>         limits.t(the jackknife object)
> would do this.  This is part of the resample library; see bottom.
My lecture notes say

  The jackknife estimate of the variance of a statistic is the variance of
  the pseudo-values divided by n. The pseudo-values could be used to give
  a confidence interval, but that has proved to be no better than the
  normal-based confidence intervals based on the mean and variance of the
  pseudo-values.

I think using t is dubiously supported if the df is small (and not much 
different from normal-based otherwise).

